        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01MkfzBKrZDC1MriuFyuFrjH",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/write-ahead-logging.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Write-Ahead Logging (WAL)\\ndescription: Database durability technique that appends changes to a log before applying them to data pages, enabling crash recovery via replay.\\ntimestamp: 2025-01-01T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Database-Durability\\n  - pattern/Write-Ahead-Logging\\n  - tech/Database-Engines\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single external source; describes the mechanism and trade-offs clearly but without detailed corroboration.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nWrite-ahead logging (WAL) is a durability mechanism in database engines that ensures data consistency across crashes and unexpected failures.\\n\\n## How It Works\\n\\nThe core principle is simple: before a database engine applies any change to its main data pages, it first appends a record of that change to a sequential log file. This strict ordering guarantees that:\\n\\n1. **Log write completes** \\u2014 the change is persisted to the log\\n2. **Page write occurs** \\u2014 the change is eventually written to the main data storage\\n\\nIf a crash happens between these two steps, the database can recover by replaying the log from the last checkpoint, reconstructing the state up to the last safe point.\\n\\n## Trade-Offs\\n\\n### Durability Gain\\nWAL trades a small amount of **write amplification** for strong durability guarantees. Each change is written twice:\\n- Once to the sequential log file\\n- Once eventually to the main data page\\n\\nThis dual-write cost is the price of eliminating the need to force a full-page flush to disk for every single transaction, which would be much slower.\\n\\n### Performance Benefit\\nBy batching page writes instead of forcing each transaction to flush immediately, WAL engines can:\\n- Amortize disk I/O across multiple transactions\\n- Batch writes more efficiently\\n- Avoid the latency of synchronous full-page flushes\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Source reference: eval://log-paragraph-specificity/sample-03\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
